Push Word onto the Stack
SP $\leftarrow$ SP $-$ 2, STACK $\leftarrow$ M1:M2
M+
This instruction pushes either a 16-bit literal value or the memory
word indicated
onto the stack, and decrements the value of the Stack Pointer by 2.

\underline{NOTE}: Unlike JSR, which leaves the most significant byte at the higher stack address, PHW pushes the least significant byte first, followed by the most significant byte. The word therefore lies on the stack with its most significant byte at the lower address (big-endian), and cannot be used directly as a little-endian pointer with stack-relative addressing. For example, PHW \#\$1234 leaves \$12 at SP+1 and \$34 at SP+2.
